    "failure_scenario": "A check like `expect_column_max_to_be_between` runs against a free-text column (e.g. `notes`) that isn't tagged/policy-classified as PII. `redact_observed_value` now correctly returns the raw cell value (`{\"observed_value\": \"some real customer note text\"}`) since the column isn't known-sensitive. That redacted dict is passed to `observed_value_exposes_cells` in `backend/app/api/v1/runs.py::_exposed_result_ids` (line 361), which only checks `isinstance(values, list)` for the observed_value shape and returns `False` for any scalar — so this raw-cell read is silently excluded from the run's exposed-result-ids list and never recorded as an access exposure, even though the equivalent list-shaped case (a raw, shown list) IS counted as exposed by the same function. The `redaction`/`redacted_columns` fields on `ResultRead` are derived solely from `sample_failures` (via `redact_sample_failures_with_state`), not from `observed_value`, so there is no other path that would catch this. G1's audit trail ('who saw raw data') therefore under-reports for any scalar-observed-value check, which is exactly the failure mode this PR's own docstring warns against ('an audit field that lies is worse than one that is missing')."
